========================================================================================
THM-841: NO DYADIC BREAKPOINT TREE; EXACT ALL-ORDER CLOSURE
========================================================================================
local lemma: i+j>k => min(floor(k/i),floor(k/j))=1
collision: lambda_(m,n)=mn/(n*j+m*i)
nonprimitive collision: lambda_(m,n)>1/(k+1)
actual lambda-breakpoints: primitive lambda=1/(i+j) only
parameter graph per Farey gap: a path with at most one kink

m_1 = sum_g [1-lambda(i+j)]_+/(ij)
m_2 = 1-lambda*sum_(d<=floor(k/2)) phi(d)/d-O_k(lambda)
m_r = 1-(2lambda/r)*sum_(d<=floor(k/r)) phi(d)/d, r>=3
O_k(lambda)=sum_g [lambda(i+j)-1]_+/(ij)
S_1 = 2k lambda
S_rho = 2lambda*sum_d phi(d)/d*sum_(m=rho..floor(k/d)) C(m-1,rho-1)/m
        + 1_(rho=2) O_k(lambda), rho>=2

structure audit: Farey gaps=27832, nonprimitive collisions=35854, k<=64
formula audit: (k,lambda) rows=359, exact m_r/S_rho equalities=8980, k<=20
formula verdict: ALL EXACT against independent circle interval sweep

DYADIC / TOOTHPICK REFUTATION
D_(2p)=D_p union (1/2)D_p union {1/m: p<m<=2p, m odd}
first local defect: D_4 \ (D_2 union (1/2)D_2) = {1/3}
actual witness gap: (0/1,1/4)
literal Farey-end additions b(1)=1, b(n)=2phi(n) for n>=2
first recurrence failure: b(5)=8, but 2b(1)+b(2)=4=A139251(5)
dyadic totals (n, Farey-end total, A139250 total):
   1:   1    1
   2:   3    3
   4:  11   11
   8:  43   43
  16: 159  171
the 1,2,4,8 agreement is a finite mirage; 159 != 171 at n=16

THM-826 CROSS-CORRECTION
for even k, denominator sum 2k-2 is absent: candidates (k-2,k),(k-1,k-1),(k,k-2)
are non-coprime/equal; it is not an actual breakpoint

ASSUMPTION CHALLENGE / TOURNAMENT ANALYSIS
vertices tested: endpoint multiple-events, spatial cells, lambda-collisions, Farey gaps
predicate preserved by the endpoint carrier: the full violation count W(t)
information destroyed by dyadic quotient: odd multipliers p<m<=2p
no tournament is formed: the exact switch relation is laminar/path-like, with no cycles or SCC choice
challenged assumption: two-sided chains can branch; Farey adjacency forces one side to be primitive-only

